WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984
Size versus statistical power.

2
00:00:02.264 --> 00:00:04.589
Your math background makes this part quick.

3
00:00:04.869 --> 00:00:14.256
A pass rate on n independent cases is an estimate of a proportion, with standard error the square root of p times (1 minus p) over n.

4
00:00:14.536 --> 00:00:17.032
Two consequences shape test-set design.

5
00:00:17.312 --> 00:00:19.701
First, what you can detect depends on n.

6
00:00:19.981 --> 00:00:25.635
A 50-case set at 80 percent has a 95 percent margin of about 11 points.

7
00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:30.544
It will catch a broken release; it won’t tell you whether a prompt tweak gained 3 points.

8
00:00:30.824 --> 00:00:33.235
The size you need depends on the decision.

9
00:00:33.515 --> 00:00:37.845
For finding failure modes, a few dozen well-chosen cases is plenty.

10
00:00:38.125 --> 00:00:43.651
For a regression gate, you need enough cases per critical behavior that one failure is meaningful.

11
00:00:43.931 --> 00:00:51.525
For comparing two models or prompts on overall quality, you typically need hundreds, or repeated trials, or a paired design.

12
00:00:51.805 --> 00:00:54.301
Second, cases aren’t always independent.

13
00:00:54.581 --> 00:01:02.795
Twenty synthetic variations of one tuple are closer to one case than to twenty, and near-duplicates from logs behave the same way.

14
00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:10.968
Deduplicate, and when you report results, report per slice as well as overall, so a big slice can’t hide a collapse in a small one.

15
00:01:11.248 --> 00:01:17.349
Week 3 turns this into confidence intervals, clustered errors for related cases, and paired tests.

16
00:01:17.629 --> 00:01:21.448
For now, keep one rule: never report a pass rate without its n.
